# UX design sheet for the SemText `npl-section` element — labelled section chrome
# Lane: tag-nitpick/npl-section. Type image: one long design sheet, one panel per display mode.
schema: "0.4"
id: npl-section-ux-design-sheet
type: image
quality: high

prompt:
  text: |
    Design sheet for a documentation-site section element called "npl-section" — a
    generic typed section that renders one small-caps label above its authored body.
    Render one long vertical design sheet on a warm off-white page, every panel
    labelled with a small monospace caption. One panel per display mode, in this order:

    1. Panel "type=\"algorithm\"" — the label ALGORITHM in all-small-caps, muted grey,
       letter-spaced, sitting above a 1px hairline rule; beneath it an ordered list body.
    2. Panel "type=\"pseudocode\"" — same chrome, label PSEUDOCODE, body is two short
       indented prose lines.
    3. Panel "type=\"diagram\"" — label DIAGRAM, body a small ASCII box diagram in
       monospace.
    4. Panel "type=\"example\"" — label EXAMPLE, body one paragraph of prose.
    5. Panel "type=\"format\"" — label FORMAT, body one paragraph.
    6. Panel "type=\"logic\"" — label LOGIC, body two prose lines.
    7. Panel "type=\"note\"" — label NOTE, body one sentence; the panel stays a plain
       labelled section, NO callout tint, NO collapse affordance, NO icon.
    8. Panel "type=\"syntax\"" — label SYNTAX, body a monospace grammar line.
    9. Panel "type=\"template\"" — label TEMPLATE, body an escaped template fragment.
    10. Panel "unknown type" — a dashed amber left border 3px wide, an amber small-caps
        label reading "MNEMONIC · UNKNOWN TYPE", body one plain sentence fully legible.
    11. Panel "class-form alias" — the same section rendered from a plain div with a
        data-type attribute: identical label and hairline chrome.
    12. Panel "JS off" — the same content with no label at all: plain prose, nothing
        generated, nothing hidden.

    Style: minimal-tech documentation design system; generous whitespace; type scale
    with 16px body, 12px small-caps labels; hairline rules at 20% grey; amber used only
    in the unknown-type panel. Include a second column or a lower strip showing the
    same twelve panels on a deep navy dark surface, labels switching to light grey and
    amber staying readable.

    Hard rules for every panel: the authored body text is ALWAYS fully legible and
    never occluded, dimmed below readable contrast, blurred, or covered; the label
    always sits ABOVE the body as the first line; panels never collapse, fold, or
    truncate; colour never carries meaning alone — the unknown-type panel names the
    problem in words, not just amber; no icons, no badges, no chrome beyond the label,
    the hairline, and (unknown panel only) the dashed rule.
  negative: >
    no hidden or occluded text, no spoiler treatment, no collapsible carets, no
    coloured-only status signals, no heavy cards or drop shadows, no rounded pill
    labels, no emoji, no decorative gradients, no invented element names
  style: >
    flat minimal technical documentation UI, small-caps micro-typography, hairline
    separators, monospace for code and captions, restrained single-accent palette

attachments:
  - path: /Users/keithbrings/Work/Space/Noizu/.tmp/semtext-docs-overhaul/snaps/npl-section/light-1280.png
    role: reference
  - path: /Users/keithbrings/Work/Space/Noizu/.tmp/semtext-docs-overhaul/snaps/npl-section/dark-1280.png
    role: reference

output:
  formats:
    - format: png
      filename: npl-section-ux-design-sheet.png
  dimensions:
    width: 1536
    height: 2304
    aspect_ratio: "2:3"

eval:
  pass_threshold: 0.8
  criteria:
    label-above-body:
      weight: 0.25
      description: >
        Every panel shows the small-caps label as the first line above the body,
        separated by a hairline rule.
      fail_signals:
        - label missing on a known-type panel
        - label rendered below or beside the body
    body-legibility:
      weight: 0.25
      description: >
        Authored body text is fully legible in all twelve panels, light and dark.
      fail_signals:
        - any body text occluded, blurred, or low-contrast
        - any panel truncating its content
    unknown-type-treatment:
      weight: 0.2
      description: >
        Only the unknown-type panel carries the dashed amber rule and names the value
        in words; other panels stay quiet.
      fail_signals:
        - amber or warning styling on a known-type panel
        - unknown panel signalling by colour alone with no text
    dark-mode-parity:
      weight: 0.15
      description: >
        Dark strip mirrors the light sheet with readable labels and amber.
      fail_signals:
        - dark panels unreadable or missing
    restraint:
      weight: 0.15
      description: >
        No invented chrome: no collapse carets, icons, badges, or callout tints on
        the note panel.
      fail_signals:
        - collapse affordance or callout styling present
        - decorative elements not in the spec
  reject_if:
    - any body text hidden or occluded in any panel
    - unknown-type panel uses colour without naming the value in words
    - labels appear below their bodies

tags:
  - semtext
  - npl-section
  - design-sheet
  - documentation-ux
